\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Peter Wohllben}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Many yellowstone wolves now wear radio collars to help researchers locate the packs and find out how they move around the park\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 or when they leave it altogether. Elli radinger, who has spent years observing wolves in yellowstone, told me that people make illegal use of these same radio signals to shoot the animals the moment they leave the protection of the park. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Deer- hiding in the undergrowth by day and come out only at night. THis tactic has worked so well that most people can hardly believe that, in relation to its size, germany is home to more large wild mammals than almost any other country on earth. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 So what is the connection between elk\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 which are strict vegetarians \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and fish? Once again, an intermediary is the key to the puzzle. In this case, the grizzly bear. Grizzly bears love to eat cutthroat trout, which have become scarce since the introduction of lake trout. Cutthroat spawn in small streams, where they are easy to catch. The invasive trout behave quite differently. They shun crystal clear tributaries and lay their eggs on the lake bed instead, which means that the exhausted parent fish endup well beyond the reach of the grizzly bears. The bruins have to find other prey to fill their rumbling stomachs. This prey is trickier to hunt and is waitng for htem on land. The bears now target elk calves, more and more of which meet tehir end after a blow from a well armored grizzly bear paw. This is now happening so much that the elk population is declining noticeably.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 After mealtime, some parts of the salmon are left lying around to fertilize the soil directly. A lot of nitrogen is also distrubted through the feces the animals expel after the feast. The amount of nitrogen that ends up in the forests alongside salmon streams is enormous. Scientists Scott M. Gende and Thosmas P. Quinn reported that according to their detailed molecular analyses, up to 70 percent of the nitrogen in vegetation growning along side the streams comes from the ocean\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 in other words, from salmon. Their data show that nitrogen from salmon speeds up the growth of trees so much tat sitka spruce in these areas grow up to three times faster than tehy would have without the fish fertilizer. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Katsuhiko Matsunaga, a marine chemist at hokkaido university, discovered that fallen leaves leach acids into streams and rivers that re then swept down to the ocean. There, the acids fuel the growth of plankton, which are the first and most important link in the food chain. More fish because of the forest?  The researcher recommended that local fishing companies plant trees along the coastline and riverbanks. More trees meant that more leaves fell into the water, and in time, increased tree cover led to increased numbers of fish and oysters for local fishing companies. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Armuthsbach or \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91stream of poverty\u8217\'92\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 is a testament to teh straitened circumstances of bygone generations.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s pretty much the same with plants. Like us, they don\u8217\'92t need nitrogen to breathe. What interests thema re the special compounds in which it is held. These compounds are reactive, and they can be broken down and converted into protein or built into plant\u8217\'92s genetic material. Unfortunately, these compounds tend to be rare in the natural world. If a tree doesn\u8217\'92t have the good fortune to be growing alongside a salmon stream, it has a problem. Feces dropped by passing animals or perhaps even a whole carcass rotting within reach of its roots are causes for celebration. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Lightning plays its part by using its energy to combine atmospheric nitrogen with oxygen to create compounds plants can break down adn absorb\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Overall, it seems, nature regards these valuable nitrogen compounds as rare treats. Then we came along. Our modern internal combustion engines, in vehicles or heating systems, do the same thing lightning does. As a by-product of burning fossil fuels, they combine atmoshperic nitrogen with oxygen to create compoudns that travel long distances on the wind all over the world and are then washed back down to earth when it rains. Then tehres agriculture, which forces the soil to be as productive as possible by adding synthetic fertilizers containing nitrogen. The quantity of nitrogen compounds released by human activity is significant. Every year, about 220 million tons worldwide rain down on the earth\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 about 60 pounds per person in the world. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Left to their own devices, trees do not grow quickly. In undisturbed ancient forests, youngsters have to spend their first two hundred years waiting patiently in their mother\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s shade. As they struggle to put on a few feet, they develop wood that is incredibly dense. In modern managed foreststoday, seedlings grow without any parental shade to slow them down. They shoot up and form large growth rings even without a nutrient boost from added nitrodgen. Consequently, their woody cells are much larger than normal and contain much more air, which mkaes them susceptible to fungi\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 after all, fungi like to breath, too. A tree that grows quickly rots quickly, and thus never has a chance to grow old.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 To protect a stand of ancient trees from loggign, we established a cemetery in the forest. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Wolves and ravens might live peaceably with one another, but other species fight over food resources. Apart from the black birds, there are other feathered parties, such as bald eagles or kites that would love to haul away a portion of the booty. With all the commotion and clamor as animals wait their turn, the ground around the carcass gets torn up. The plants are shuffled, because seeds that would otherwise have been stifled in the matted grass now have their moment in the sun. }{\f1\fs24\b1\i0\cf1 Things also change for vegetation that is not disturbed. Rotting flesh serves as fertilizer\hich\f1 \emdash \loch\f1 for the plants, deer carcasses are simply overgrown salmon. THe more robust growth and greener color of the grass and nonwoody plants for about 3 feet around the carcass are evidence of the nutrient boost.}
\par\plain \f1\fs24\b1\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And what happens to the bones? After the felsh has been eaten or has rotted away, there should be huge numbers of bones lying around in field and forest, bleaching in the sun. But no,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There are two forces at work here. Sick or weak animals separate themselves from others of their kind to hide in the undergrowth, or on hot summer days they wander near or into small streams to cool any wounds they might have. Here, they wait for death. That makes sense, because this way they don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t endanger their kin\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 weak animals attract the attention of predators. Also, in a secluded spot there\u8217\'92s no one to disturb them in their final hours. Usually, it is our sense of smell that leads us to dead animals in places like this; bones lie quietly hidden from sight under bushes. Because bones basically don\u8217\'92t break down, and because every now and then animals certainly die away from the protective cover of vegetation, over time you\u8217\'92d expect to find bones scattered all over the place. But that is not the case, because there are plenty of takers for dead animals final remains\u8230\'85.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Take the bone skipper, which disappeared without at trace until it was rediscovered in 2009. This bizarre insect with its tiny orangey-red head looks like a creature froma fantasy world, and it doesn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t behave like other flies, either. The bone skipper likes it nice and cold. It is out and about mostly on winter nights, on the lookout for dead anim als and cracked bones. Here, it feasts and lays its eggs. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Unlike us, these nocturnal hunters make a series of short, rapid calls\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 about a hundred a second. And with bats, its all about volume: the sounds they make can be as loud as 130 decibels, which would hurt our ears if we could hear them (but ultrasounds are out of our range). Unlike lower frequency sounds, ultrasounds are quickly swallowed up in the air, and after they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve traveled about 300 feet, there\u8217\'92s not much left to hear. Even so, on summer nights it can get quite noisy out in forests and fields\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 in the upper frequencies at least.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 With the moon, that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s not a problem because it\u8217\'92s almost infinitely far away. With the light, which is close by, the insecct quickly flies past. The source of the light is now behind it. It keeps correcting course, and its course corrections lead it to fly in an ever-diminishing circle. Eventaully, the moth crashes right into the light itself. It keeps starting again to try and get away\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and ever new attempt at escape ends in failure. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Some moths die of exhaustion; others meet their end more quickly. Over time, many bats have specialized in patrolling streetlamps.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 Are not  humans so different? -e.g. bluelight fuckery. But also social media fuckery.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f1\fs24\b1\i0\cf1 Many dead deer were found, which heated up the debate: were we really going to allow these noble animals to starve?When veterinarians examined the animals, however, they made an unexpected discovery. They found the victims\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92 stomachs were stuffed full of food, which ruled out hunger as the cause of death. They also found enormous numbers of gut and stomach parasites, and these were what had sealed the deer\u8217\'92s fate. Because of the increased numbers of deer, the animasl had more contact with each other and with contaminated feces, which helped parasites spread widely. Once ingested, the parasites, not the deer, were the beneficiaries of the nutrients in the deer\u8217\'92s guts, which meant that many deer weakened and died, even though they had plenty to eat\hich\f1 \emdash \loch\f1 death by starvation was an indirect consequence of the feeding program.}
\par\plain \f1\fs24\b1\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 When an ancient tree dies, grasses and bushes grow in the clearing that opens up around it, gradually creating a small grassy plain where browsers move in to eat all the small trees that try to sprout. Trees, however, know how to put a stop to that, and one way they put the brakes on meadow making is to leave long intervals between t he times they bloom in order to reduce the number of browsers in the neighborhood. But that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s not all. What use is it if only some of the trees take a break while others are loaded with beechnuts and acorns? Wild boar go hungry only when there are no nutritious seeds anywhere for at least a few years.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ANd so the trees come up with a communal bloom strategy, and all trees of the same species have to be on board. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s not enough , for example, for one stand of beeches to come to an agreement via their root and fungal connections in the ground. That form of communication works well (and, astonishngly enough, some of it is via electrical impulses), but the wood wide web doesn\u8217\'92t reach far enough for this particular purpose, because wild boar can roam long distances and simply find another stand of trees 6 to 12 miles away. Therefore, trees agree among themselves over long distances, and by longdistances I mean hundreds of miles. We don\u8217\'92t yet know exaclty how this works\u8230\'85.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85the fungi also help trees reach essential minerals. For example, when trees suck up phosphorus compounds, they soon exhaust the supply within a few fractions of an inch of their roots. So its a good thing that fungi connected to the larger network spin their filaments around the trees\u8217\'92 delicate feeder roots to extend their reach. That way, ll the nutrients the trees need can be delivered to their door even from distant parts of the forest floor.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The fruiting bodies are mostly constructed as a stalk with a cap on top, and there\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a good reason for that, as biomatheematitican marcus roper discovered. The spores trickle out of openings on the underside of the cap where they are sheltered from the rain so that they don\u8217\'92t get wet and clump together. The cap itself transpires watorvapor, cooling the air around the mushroom just a little bit. The cooled air around the edge of the capsinks slightly, taking the spores with it, and then warms up again in the surrounding air. Both the warmed air and the spores now waft out and up about 4 inches above the cap. All it takes is a tiny breeze to carry the tiny passengers away, adn the survival of ceps and co is assured.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The wood thickens because the tree is rushing to heal itself. If it dallies, fungi and insects will have a better chance of brazenly pushing their way inside. In the chaos, the tree has no time to worry about neatly organized fibers, and at first that doesn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t matter. After a few years, the  task is done. The wound has healed over, though there will always be a thick scar to show where a deer or tractor hurt the tree. But just because the trauma is over doesn\u8217\'92t mean its forgotten. Now it all depends on what other stresses come along. One day, cold temperatures arrive and our veteran tree is at a distinct disadvantage as the damp sapwood inside it freezes solid and the ice threatens to shatter its trunk.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Healing the old wound has resulted in a chaotic bundle of fibers that exert varing amounts of pressure on the wood around them when they freeze. On clear, frosty nights, cracking noises reverberate like rifle shots through the forest..the woody tissue fails around the old wound and splits open so abruptly that the sound travels for miles in a phenomenon known as frost cracking.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
